  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remnants of Hurricane Isaias to bring rain, \nbreezy conditions Sunday into Sunday night.\n\nThe pattern remains quiet through the first half of the weekend\nas surface high pressure strengthens over the northeastern U.S.\nHowever, this lengthy period of tranquil weather will come to an\nend as this ridge retreats northward ahead of Isaias. Until\nthen, a thick area of high cirrus should continue to track\npoleward. Daily highs will be in the low/mid 70s, with 60s\nacross mountain locations.\n\nThe main weather impact to the region over the next week is going to \nbe from the remnants of what is now Hurricane Isaias (Cat 3\nstrength). Model guidance is in generally good agreement that \nHurricane Isaias' surface low weakens and broadens as the system\nmoves into the TN Valley Saturday night into Sunday. The mid-\nlevel circulation amplifies and becomes a strong cutoff low over\nKY/TN that moves slowly east across the Mid- Atlantic Sunday \ninto Monday. Periods of rain are likely across the area at times\nSaturday evening through Sunday night. Rainfall amounts of 1-2 \ninches are expected across the area. Higher rain totals are \ncertainly possible, but will depend on a few other smaller scale\nfeatures.\n\nTropical moisture surges north into our area Saturday night into \nSunday, with the highest PWAT values at or above 2\" (most likely \neast of the Blue Ridge). While the main cutoff low associated with \nthe remnants of Isaias remain west of the Appalachians on Saturday, \nexpect light rain to move in from south to north in the evening. \nForcing for ascent increases Saturday night, and especially Sunday \nmorning into Sunday afternoon as mid-level heights fall with the \napproach of the cutoff low. There is going to be enhanced low-level \nconvergence somewhere over the region as the surface high to the \neast and cutoff low to the west interact. Easterly flow into the \nBlue Ridge could also produce enhanced rain amounts due to \norographic effects. Additionally, an inverted trough or wave of low \npressure tries to develop in lee of the Appalachians Sunday as it \ncrosses Virginia to the Delmarva.\n\nUncertainty remains regarding the exact evolution and timing of \nthese features, which will ultimately determine where / if any \nlocally heavy rain occurs. Continue to monitor the forecast over the \nnext couple of days. No Flood Watches are planned at this time as we \nare still over 48 hours from the main slug of rain.\nAdditionally, there is a non-zero risk of isolated tornadoes on\nSunday. These post-tropical environments can be tricky as low-\nlevel spin ups do occur. For now, the Storm Prediction Center\nhas maintained a Day 3 Marginal Risk area.\n\nIt is going to be noticeably breezy, especially in the mountains \nwhere winds begin to pick up Saturday evening. The strongest winds \nare most likely on Sunday. The current forecast for peak wind gusts \nis 30-40 mph in the Alleghenies and 20-30 mph elsewhere.\n\nConditions dry out Monday morning into Monday afternoon as the \nremnants of Isaias push offshore. Looking at mostly mild \ntemperatures for most of next week. A series of cold fronts cross \nthe region during the middle to late part of next week as the \nsynoptic pattern becomes more active. Aside from mountain rain \nshowers, most of the area looks to remain dry. The strongest cold \nfront arrives late next week, possibly bringing rain and colder air \nbehind it.",
